1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Elie de Saussure''' was the oldest surviving son of Jean-Baptiste de Saussure and wife Suzanne Diodati. He was born in 1602, probably in Geneva. In 1626, he inherited the title of Seigneur and the estate of Ches
2 CONC eaux, in Vaud, Switzerland, upon the death of his uncle Daniel de Saussure. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1633, Elie married Sara Burlamacchi at the Eglise Italienne in Geneva. The Burlamacchi were Italian Calvinist refugees from the Lucca area, and were closely allied with the Diodati family. Sara's mother was Anne Diodati and her
2 CONC  aunt Madeleine married Jean Diodati. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1635, Elie was considered to be bourgeoisie of Geneva and lived a comfortable life on Rue de le Poissonnerie. Elie and Sara had thirteen children. The oldest surviving son, Cesar, was baptized in 1637 at the Eglise Italienne. B
2 CONC y 1647, the year his father died, Elie had gained a seat on the Council of Twenty-Five, a group of magistrates who yielded immense power in Geneva.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elie's line is the only one that survived to the present day. His European descendants use the spelling de Saussure or, in modern times, simply Saussure. His youngest son, Henri, had a grandson also named Henri, who emigrated to S
2 CONC outh Carolina in the early 1700's.  Henri's descendants spell the name De Saussere or de Saussure.
