1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Swiss/Italian descent. From Tanap, notes to this entry-date not specified. V.C. 12, pp 562-564 print version.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maandag den 4n December. D'luijtenant Nicholaas de Jonge van Oosterland ende boekhouer Roelof Diodati, (1) tot  t'opnemen van's Comps, buijtenposten en d' ommeslag der vrijluijden. (1) Kom in die monsterrol van 1692 as Roelof Diod
2 CONC ati van Dorth, wat as boekhouer 30 per maand verdien het. Hy hey by die Kaap egter op 24 Augustus 1692 met die Fluitskip Duijfie verlaat met die opdrag om Isaak Johannes lamotius, opperhebber van Mauritius, gevange te neem en hom 
2 CONC op te volg. Hy was to 1703 opperhoof van genoemde eilaand en het later as koopman en boekhouer van die ambagskwaatier in Batavia en daarna opperhoof van die Kompanjie se handelspos in Dejima Japan geword. Volgens sy handtekening i
2 CONC s bostaande spelling, naamlik Roelof Diodati, die juiste spelling.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liebbrandt, letters dispatched 1695-1708, Diodati seems to have been replaced by Momber circa 1703 and to have left Mauritius for Batavia 1704.
2 CONT
2 CONT * (Crampton, Hazel (2014) ''The Side Of The Sun At Noon''. South Africa: Jacana). 
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Roelof Diodati
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Mauritius
2 CONT
2 CONT Roelof Diodati (Dordrecht, 28 July 1658 – Batavia, 10 March 1723) was a governor of Dutch Mauritius in the late 17th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Diodati was from Swiss-Italian descent. His grandfather was Jean Diodati, a theologian, who translated the Bible in Italian. His father, born in Geneva, became a pastor of the Walloon church in Leiden in 1651.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not obvious Rodolfo Diodati was one of a twin.[2] Both brothers took service at the Dutch East India Company. He became an accountant at the Cape from 1686 and then a merchant. He was appointed as governor of Mauritius from 
2 CONC 1692-1703. In 1693 he had to deal with François Leguat.[3] In 1695, a big hurricane devastated the island, several of the Burghers lost all theirs crops, many left the island.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Diodati seems to have been appointed in Suratte. Then he shifted to Batavia and he became a merchant and accountant on 4 January 1707. In 1709 he married Catharina Zaaiman, born on Dutch Mauritius. Her grandmother was Eva, a Khoik
2 CONC hoi interpreter for Jan van Riebeeck.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Diodati became opperhoofd at the VOC post at Dejima Japan on 31 May 1720 and died in Batavia 10 March 1723.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT [1] The Voyage of Franois Leguat of Bresse, to Rodriguez, Mauritius, Java, and ... By François Le Guat [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT [2] Akte 41
2 CONT
2 CONT [3] Mauritius illustrated: historical and descriptive, commercial and industrial ... By Allister Macmillan [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT [4] http://www.colonialvoyage.com/mauritius.html
2 CONT
2 CONT [5] http://www.zaaiman.nl/Stamboom/Zuid-Afrika/Zuid-Afrika.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Allister Macmillan, Mauritius illustrated: historical and descriptive, commercial and industrial facts, figures, and resources., London : W.H. & L. Collingridge, 1914
2 CONT
2 CONT Bygevoeg deur Y. DROST, 17 JAN 2016
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT MAURITIUS. The Star and the Key of the Indian Ocean by Dr. Catherine Millard. 2002. ISBN: 978 99903 36 12 C
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 3
2 CONT
2 CONT Governor Roëlof Deodati arrived in Mauritius on October 22nd, 1692. The following year, Leguat and his companions left Rodriques for Mauritius, disembarking at Black River on May 29th. However, on January 15th, 1694, Francois Legu
2 CONC at and his Huguenot colleagues were arrested by Governor Deodati, and exiled on Frederik Hendrik Isle (Ile aux Fouquets). [Source: Chelin, Antoine. Maurice: Une Ile et son Passe. Ile de la Reunion: Edition du Centre de Recherche I
2 CONC ndianoceanique, 1989; p. 3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bygevoeg deur Y. DROST, 17 JAN 2016
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT National Heritage Museum in Mahebourg, Mauritius
2 CONT
2 CONT On display the following note (See photos in Media tab):-
2 CONT
2 CONT Speaker
2 CONT
2 CONT These artifacts have been found on the Speaker commanded by the pirate John Bowen who was born in England in 1660 and died in Bourbon in 1705. His activities seem to have been confined to the Indian Ocean where they extended from 
2 CONC Madagascar to Bengal. He captured in 1700, the Speaker, a French vessel, owned by an English company interested in the slave trade. On the 7 January 1702 he lost his ship at the mouth of Grand River South East, in Mauritius and wa
2 CONC s well treated by the Dutch governor Deodati whose little force of 52 men would, in any case, have been no match for Bowen's 170 well armed and trained ruffians. Before the Speaker was wrecked, John Bowen captured and robbed the E
2 CONC nglish vessel "Borneo" and two Moorish ships.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bowen (1660-1705)
2 CONT
2 CONT After three months' hospitality on the island, Bowen procured a ship and, on 24 march 1702, sailed for Madagascar. As a parting gift to Deodati, he gave him 2,500 pieces of eight and the wreck of the Speaker, with all the guns and
2 CONC  stores. He left also twelve English and 30 Moorish prisoners taken from a captured ship. Two of Bowen crew, Israël Phipeny and Peter Freeland also remained in Mauritius whence they found their way back to Portsmouth in 1725.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Bygevoeg op 5 DES 2022 deur Y. DROST)
2 CONT
