1 NAME Charles Pictet /de Rochemont/
2 GIVN Charles Pictet
2 _MARNM de Rochemont
1 NAME Charles Pictet de Rochemont
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 21 SEP 1755
2 ADDR
3 CITY Cartigny
3 CTRY Switzerland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 28 DEC 1824
2 ADDR
3 CITY Lancy
3 CTRY Switzerland
1 BURI
2 DATE JAN 1825
2 PLAC Cimetière des Rois (Cemetery of the Kings)
2 ADDR
3 ADR1 1204 Genève
3 CITY Geneva
3 CTRY Switzerland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Barbara Roth: "Pictet, Charles", in: Historisches Lexikon der Schweiz (HLS), Version vom 04.08.2010, übersetzt aus dem Französischen. Online: https://hls-dhs-dss.ch/de/articles/020517/2010-08-04/, konsultiert am 23
2 CONC .07.2022
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/28322133/charles-pictet_de_rochemont
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Pictet was born on 21 September 1755 at Cartigny near Geneva into an aristocratic but tolerant family. He is the son of a colonel, Charles Pictet (1713–1792) and of Marie, born Dunant. Since his young age, he wanted to ded
2 CONC icate himself to a military career. At the age of 20 he went to France and for twelve years pursued a career in the French Army. After his marriage in 1786 to Adélaïde Sara de Rochemont he modified his name to the more aristocrati
2 CONC c "Pictet de Rochemont". Two years later he entered the governing council of Geneva and was made responsible for reorganizing the urban militia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1792 the former city councils of Geneva were suspended and a provisional government took over, declaring all citizens equal. In 1794 Pictet was placed under house arrest for a year. His father-in-law, Jean-François de Rochemont
2 CONC , was less lucky; he was executed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Napoleonic Wars:
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, Switzerland became a theatre of war. Napoleon had invaded in 1798 and everywhere egalitarian ideas took root and the old aristocracy was swept away. Armies devastated the countryside and the winter of 1800 would long be rem
2 CONC embered as one of misery and starvation. Geneva was annexed to France. A few weeks later Napoleon issued a new constitution for a Helvetic Republic trying to replace the former archaic system of cantonal authorities with a central
2 CONC ized executive. This caused such a violent reaction that Napoleon withdrew his troops in 1802.
2 CONT
2 CONT The power vacuum thus created set off a civil war, during which Napoleon offered his services as arbitrator. He urged the Swiss to come up with a constitution themselves. The new constitution restored the notion of autonomous cant
2 CONC ons, created six new cantons and gave a new name to the country - the Swiss Confederation. Meanwhile, in 1798, Pictet de Rochemont had acquired seventy-five hectares of land at Lancy, centred on the present mairie de Lancy, and le
2 CONC d the life of a gentleman farmer. He concentrated on the breeding of merino sheep and introduced the culture of maize to the Geneva region. His agrarian innovations spread. Alongside his life as a farmer, he wrote a great deal and
2 CONC  founded a review entitled La bibliothèque britannique. From 1796 to 1815 he wrote the agricultural column in it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Napoleon’s downfall led to the liberation of Geneva by Austrian troops. A new republican government was declared on 1 January 1814 and Pictet read the public proclamation, probably written by himself. Napoleon’s brief return to po
2 CONC wer in 1815 - the Hundred Days - ending at the Battle of Waterloo does not seem to have had any effect on the events that followed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Diplomat:
2 CONT
2 CONT Two apparently contradictory objectives of the new government, much favoured by Pictet, were to restore Genevan independence but also to make Geneva part of the Swiss Confederation. To achieve this it was necessary to: (a) to make
2 CONC  Genevan territory homogenous (it consisted of several fragmented communes); and (b) to connect it physically to the canton of Vaud and thus to Switzerland as a whole (Versoix was in France). Pictet participated in the first deput
2 CONC ation sent in 1814 to request that the Great Powers support Geneva’s position. He then represented Geneva and Switzerland in several rounds of meetings held in Paris (Treaty of Paris) and Vienna (Congress of Vienna) during 1814 an
2 CONC d 1815. While the victors were mainly interested in sharing the spoils of war, Pictet de Rochement’s political talent and diplomatic skills were aimed precisely at recovering Geneva’s independence and joining it to the twenty-one 
2 CONC cantons then forming the Swiss Confederation, Valais and Neuchâtel having just entered as full and equal cantons.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first confrontation in Paris was not a success since the French negotiator, Talleyrand, refused to let go of any part of the Pays de Gex. Later, in October 1814 there was the Congress of Vienna. Pictet participated actively in
2 CONC  the relevant conclaves and negotiations, not waiting for suitable outcomes to come his way. The idea that Geneva should form part of the Swiss Confederation became a reality on 19 May 1815.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Geneva had been not yet been able to consolidate its fragmented territory. But now, following the change in Geneva’s status, Pictet could count on the backing of the Swiss Government and was given full powers to negotiate
2 CONC . He soon achieved the territorial success he sought.
2 CONT
2 CONT North of the lake, six communes were transferred from the pays de Gex, thus giving Geneva its connection with the canton of Vaud. As had already been decided in Vienna and Paris, by the Treaty of Turin in 1816 on the left bank ano
2 CONC ther twenty-three communes were transferred from Savoy and became part of the canton. King Victor Emmanuel I of Sardinia had himself only just recently recovered this territory. This extension of the cantonal land became known as 
2 CONC the "communes réunies", hence the road of this name in Grand-Lancy. It was also stated that the non-Swiss customs posts were to be situated at least one league (approximately five kilometres) from the new Swiss frontier, thus crea
2 CONC ting the "zone franche" on both sides of the canton. Into the bargain, the Great Powers meeting in Paris recognized the "permanent neutrality of Switzerland" and agreed that Swiss neutrality was, indeed, in the common interest of 
2 CONC all European countries. Pictet himself wrote the text of the declaration of neutrality.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later life and posterity:
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1816 Pictet-de-Rochemont returned to his sheep and his maize fields having perfectly succeeded in his mission. The Swiss parliament or Diet (Tagsatzung), as it was at that time, expressed its recognition of his se
2 CONC rvices.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 28 December 1824 at Lancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT A statue of Charles Pictet de Rochemont has been erected Rampe de la Treille at the entrance of the Old City in 1970.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference:  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Pictet_de_Rochemont
2 CONT
