1 NAME Guillaume /Mallet/
2 GIVN Guillaume
2 _MARNM Mallet
1 NAME /Mallet/
2 GIVN
2 SURN Mallet
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 29 JUL 1747
2 PLAC Noisy, 93
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 MAR 1826
2 PLAC Paris
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Banker
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Guillaume was the grandson of Isaac Mallet, founder of the Mallet bank created in 1713 in Paris. He joined the family bank at an early age, which was then managed by his father, Jacques Mallet (1724-1815). He becam
2 CONC e a partner in 1775 and succeeded his father, along with his brother Jean-Jacques Mallet and a cousin.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the French Revolution although the law authorised Guillaume to pay in devalued assignats, in 1792 the Banque Mallet gave the proceeds of the sale of a town house belonging to Henri Bertin, an exiled former minister of Forei
2 CONC gn Affairs under Louis XV to M. Bertini. As a result, Guillaume and his brother were suspected of helping the émigrés – all the more so since both were bankers of former Geneva citizenship. Accused of counter-revolutionary activit
2 CONC ies, they were arrested and jailed during the Terreur in 1794. They were set free ten days after 9 Thermidor An II. During the brothers’ imprisonment, their wives’ courage avoided the closing down of the bank, threatened as it was
2 CONC  with bankruptcy.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the 9th Thermidor, the banker Sabatier tried, together with two shareholders, Guillaume Mallet and Louis-Victor Moreau, who had been appointed interim directors, to obtain the return of the seized goods of the French East In
2 CONC dia Company. Recovering only three ships, the shareholders decided the final liquidation of the Company in July 1795.
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume became a stockbroker at the Paris Stock Exchange in the year IX.
2 CONT
2 CONT He took part in the creation of the Banque de France in 1800 and became one of its first regents (he sat as regent until his death). He also joined the "Négociants réunis", which was in charge of finding funds for the Treasury. He
2 CONC  was then entrusted by Napoleon Bonaparte with the drafting of new statutes for the Banque de France following the crisis of 1805-1806.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a member of the Consistory of the Reformed Church of Paris from 1803 to 1826.
2 CONT
2 CONT He became a member of the General Council of the Seine.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1810, he was made Knight of the Legion of Honor and Baron of the Empire (title confirmed by King Louis XVIII in 1815).
2 CONT
2 CONT He took part in the creation of the Savings Bank in 1818, alongside the bank Delessert.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1779, he married Elisabeth Boy de La Tour, daughter of Pierre Boy de La Tour, silk merchant and banker in Lyon, and Julie Roguin, Rousseau's patron. Elisabeth's sister was the mother of Benjamin Delessert.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Widowed, he remarried in 1786 to Anne-Julie Houël, daughter of Louis Houël and Marthe-Julie Feray, from whom were born his two sons, James and Jules, who married the sisters Laure and Émilie Oberkampf, daughters of Christophe-Phil
2 CONC ippe Oberkampf.
2 CONT
2 CONT
