1 OCCU Kommissaris der Marine Ned. Indie 
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Born in 1778 at the fortified Dutch outpost of Jaffnapatnam on Ceylon, he entered the world just as the Dutch East India Company (VOC) was in deep financial decline. Daily life in northern Ceylon was structured by 
2 CONC strict colonial hierarchies, and children of Dutch administrators or merchants often moved between colonial and Dutch urban society. His later description as a *koopman* fits the commercial profile of many VOC-connected families. 
2 CONC After the British captured Ceylon in 1796, many Dutch families relocated to the Netherlands, which may explain his marriage in Delft in 1798, a city still shaped by the political upheaval of the Batavian Republic.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the early nineteenth century he and his family lived in The Hague, a political center where ministries and naval offices were reorganizing after the Napoleonic occupation. His later appointment as *Kommissaris der Marine* i
2 CONC n the Netherlands Indies placed him within the administrative structure that replaced the VOC after 1800, as the colonial state expanded its bureaucracy under Dutch rule from Batavia. His knighthood suggests recognition within thi
2 CONC s evolving government service, which often rewarded officers for loyalty during a turbulent period marked by wars with Britain and shifting control of Asian colonies. His death in Batavia in 1820 occurred during years when disease
2 CONC , especially malaria and dysentery, was a constant threat to European officials living in the port city.
