1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Wikipedia Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT <strong>Admiral Sir Thomas Sabine Pasley, 2nd Baronet KCB</strong> (1804–1884) was an officer of the British Royal Navy during the nineteenth century who never saw action but served across the globe in numerous positions. A career
2 CONC  officer, Pasley inherited his grandfather Sir Thomas Pasley, 1st Baronet's title aged only four and spent much of his career in shore appointments as he paid for and cared for his large family.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Major John Sabine of the Grenadier Guards and Maria Pasley, daughter of admiral Sir Thomas Pasley, 1st Baronet, a long-serving officer who had lost a leg at the Glorious First of June. As Pasley had no male heirs
2 CONC , his baronetcy would have become extinct but for an act of parliament permitting the title to fall to his grandson on his death. When Pasley senior died in 1808, his four year old grandson became Sir Thomas and added the Pasley t
2 CONC o his surname.
2 CONT
2 CONT Young Pasley was schooled at Dr. Pearson's School in East Sheen and subsequently Burnfoot School in Durham. In 1817, aged 14, Pasley attended the Royal Naval Academy and the following year joined Sir Thomas Fremantle's ship HMS Ro
2 CONC chfort as a midshipman under the patronage of his relation Pulteney Malcolm. The ship was later commanded by Sir Graham Moore. In 1823, Pasley moved to the brig HMS Redpole and later HMS Arachne. In 1824 he was promoted to lieuten
2 CONC ant and joined HMS Tweed for service off the Brazilian Coast.
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning in 1826, Pasley married Jane Matilda Lily Wynyard and the couple would ultimately have seven sons and two daughters, putting great financial strain on Pasley and once his sons entered the Navy, forcing him to provide pat
2 CONC ronage for all of them by doing favours for other officers. As a result his career would stalemate. In 1828, Pasley was promoted to commander and he joined HMS Cameleon and HMS Procris in command. As acting captain he also command
2 CONC ed the frigates HMS Rattlesnake and HMS Blonde.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pasley was made full captain in 1831, and spent several years on the Brazilian station in HMS Curacoa. In 1848, after a period of unemployment he took over Pembroke Dockyard and in 1856 he was made rear-admiral and commanded HMS R
2 CONC oyal Albert and HMS Agamemnon in the Black Sea at the end of the Crimean War. On his return in 1859 Pasley commanded Devonport Dockyard and in 1866 became commander-in-chief at Portsmouth. His lengthy shore commands were the resul
2 CONC t of the pressures maintaining his large family placed him under.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1869, Pasley retired as a full admiral and in 1873 was made a Knight Commander of the Order of the Bath. He died in 1884 and was succeeded in the baronetcy by his grandson Thomas Edward Sabine Pasley, his eldest son having died
2 CONC  in 1870. He was buried in Shedfield Chuchyard.
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: Wikipedia contributors, 'Sir Thomas Sabine Pasley, 2nd Baronet', Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia, 5 April 2012, 10:22 UTC, <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Sir_Thomas_Sabine_Pasley,_2nd_Baronet&oldid=485692727>
2 CONC  [accessed 7 February 2013]
2 CONT
2 CONT == Other References ==
