1 NAME William Jones /Boone/
2 GIVN William Jones
2 SURN Boone
2 _MARNM Boone
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Shantung Road Cemetery, Shanghai, Shanghai Municipality, China
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 7 JAN 1811
2 ADDR
3 CITY Walterboro
3 STAE South Carolina
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 17 JUL 1864
2 ADDR
3 CITY Shanghai
3 STAE Shanghai
3 CTRY China
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Jones_Boone_(father)
2 CONT
2 CONT William Jones Boone (1 July 1811 – 17 July 1864) was the first Anglican missionary bishop of Shanghai.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Boone was born in Walterboro, South Carolina, graduated from the College of South Carolina in 1829 and was admitted to the bar in 1833. He then attended Virginia Theological Seminary and was ordained deacon on 18 September 1836 an
2 CONC d priest on 3 March 1837.
2 CONT
2 CONT Missionary work in China
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the auspices of the Protestant Episcopal Church Mission, Boone was appointed a missionary to China on 17 January 1837. Accompanied by his wife Amelia he commenced his journey to China from Boston on 8 July 1837 reaching Bata
2 CONC via on 22 October the same year. In Batavia he studied alongside the priests Henry Lockwood and Francis Hanson to gain a degree of fluency in the Chinese language.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prior to the conclusion of the First Opium War Boone relocated to Macau. In February 1842 conditions in China were considered secure enough for Boone to relocate his missionary work to Kulang-see, a small island half a mile from t
2 CONC he recently opened treaty port of Amoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT On a return visit the United States Boone was consecrated at St. Peter's Church, Philadelphia on 26 October 1844 as the first Anglican missionary bishop of Shanghai. Influenced by British CMS missionary George Smith he chose to re
2 CONC locate the center of his mission work to Shanghai in 1845 where he served until his death in 1864.
2 CONT
2 CONT Boone was responsible for the recruitment of numerous missionaries; notably Emma Jones, Henry M. Parker and Channing Moore Williams his eventual successor as Bishop of Shanghai. Boone with others is credited with the translation o
2 CONC f the Book of Common Prayer into Chinese and also contributed to a Chinese translation of the Bible.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Sarah Amelia deSaussure. Died at Amoy in 1842. Second marriage to Phobe Caroline Elliott Boone's son, also named William Jones Boone, also served as a bishop of Shanghai in the Episcopal Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Boone was born in Walterborough, South Carolina, graduated from the College of South Carolina in 1829 and was admitted to the bar in 1833. He then attended Virginia Theological Seminary and was ordained deacon on 18 September 1836
2 CONC  and priest on 3 March 1837. He was appointed a missionary to China on 17 January 1837, and consecrated in October 1844 in Philadelphia as the first missionary bishop of China, where he served until his death in Shanghai 1864. He 
2 CONC translated the Book of Common Prayer into Chinese and worked on a Chinese translation of the Bible.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/81408137/william-jones-boone Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 3 2020, 23:52:24 UTC''
2 CONT ----
