1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * From https://www.wikiwand.com/en/H%C3%A9l%C3%A8ne_de_Pourtal%C3%A8s:
2 CONT ** "'''Hélène de Pourtalès'''
2 CONT *** "Countess Hélène de Pourtalès (April 28, 1868 – November 2, 1945), born Helen Barbey, was an American-born sailor who competed in the 1900 Summer Olympics representing Switzerland and became the first woman to win an Olympic g
2 CONC old medal. She was also the first woman to represent Switzerland at the Olympics. 
2 CONT ** "'''Early life'''
2 CONT *** "Helen Barbey was born on April 28, 1868, in New York City, the daughter of Henry Isaac Barbey and Mary (née Lorillard) Barbey. Her maternal grandparents were Pierre Lorillard III and Catherine Anne (née Griswold) Lorillard. H
2 CONC er sister Eva was married to André Poupart, Baron de Neuflize in 1903, the older brother of Roberte Ponsonby, Countess of Bessborough. Her father, a financier and a director of the Buffalo, Rochester and Pittsburgh Railway, was a 
2 CONC nephew of Adrian Georg Iselin and cousin of Charles Oliver Iselin.
2 CONT *** "Her family included her uncle Pierre Lorillard IV; aunt Catherine Lorillard; uncle George Lyndes Lorillard, who married Marie Louise La Farge, the daughter[citation needed] of John La Farge and the sister[citation needed] of 
2 CONC Christopher Grant La Farge, who later became the Countess de Agreda after she married Count de Agreda; and Louis Lasher Lorillard, who married Katherine Livingston Beeckman, sister of Governor Robert Livingston Beeckman.
2 CONT *** "Barbey grew up at 17 West 38th Street in New York City.
2 CONT ** "'''Career'''
2 CONT *** "De Pourtalès was a crewmember of the Swiss boat Lérina, which won the gold medal in the first race of 1–2 ton class and silver medal in the second race of 1–2 ton class. She also participated in the open class but did not fin
2 CONC ish. Her husband Hermann, as helmsman, and her husband's nephew Bernard were also crew members. De Pourtalès was also one of the first women to take part in the Olympics, as that was the first time women were allowed to compete. S
2 CONC he was very well known after her gold medal, becoming the first woman to win a gold medal two months before Charlotte Cooper.[citation needed]
2 CONT ** "'''Personal life'''
2 CONT *** "On 25 April 1891, de Pourtalès was married to Hermann Alexander, Count von Pourtalès (1847–1904), after the death of his first wife, Marguerite Marcet. Hermann was a captain of the Cuirassiers of the Guard.
2 CONT *** "From his first marriage, de Pourtalès became the stepmother of Count Guy de Pourtalès (1881–1941), the author, and Count Raimond Pourtalès (1882–1914), attache of the German embassy, who married Countess Luise Alexandra von B
2 CONC ernstorff (1888–1971), daughter of Johann Heinrich von Bernstorff, the German Ambassador to the United States in 1911. The wedding, which took place in Washington, D.C. was attended by William Howard Taft, who was then the Preside
2 CONC nt of the United States. After Raimond's death in 1914, his widow Luise Alexandra remarried to Prince Johannes Baptista of Löwenstein-Wertheim-Rosenberg (1880–1956), the youngest son of Charles, 6th Prince of Löwenstein-Wertheim-R
2 CONC osenberg.
2 CONT *** "De Pourtalès died on November 2, 1945, in Geneva."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.geni.com/people/Helen-de-Pourtal%C3%A8s/6000000189679949880
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter of Henry Isaac Barbey (1832-1906), a Swiss-American banker living in New York, and granddaughter of Pierre Lorillard III, she married Hermann de Pourtalès in 1891.
2 CONT She participated in the 1900 Summer Olympics in Paris; her results were counted for Switzerland. On board the Lérina with her husband Hermann and her nephew Bernard, she competed in the two races of class 1 - 2 barrels. The Swiss 
2 CONC crew won the gold medal after the first race and the silver medal after the second race.
2 CONT Her son-in-law Guy de Pourtalès, from her husband's first marriage, is a writer.
