1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Louis M. DeSaussure was born 20 May 1804 in Prince William Parish, South Carolina.  His father was Henry W. DeSaussure, who was elected to the chancery bench in 1808 and served the state as chancellor for the next 
2 CONC 29 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis DeSaussure was a physician and planter in Beaufort County, South Carolina.  He appears to have owned two principal properties, one the Beaufort County plantation, and the other a home called "Woodstock."  Judging from DeSaus
2 CONC sure's slave lists, the plantation was a moderately large operation, with cotton as its primary crop.
2 CONT
2 CONT DeSaussure was a member of the Episcopal Church.  He married Jane Hay Hutson, with whom he had two sons:  Charles Alfred (1846-1935), who was also a physician and who, in 1930, served as commander-in-chief of the United Confederat
2 CONC e Veterans; and Thomas Hutson, who, for forty years, was resident engineer at the State Insane Sanatarium at Milledgeville, Georgia.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Civil War, DeSaussure first served, with his son Charles, as surgeon with the 8th South Carolina Regiment. Later he appears to have been surgeon with the 4th South Carolina Regiment.  After the war, his property having 
2 CONC been confiscated, DeSaussure settled in Camden, South Carolina, where he practiced medicine until his death on 6 June 1869.
