1 NAME Harrington /Mann/
2 GIVN Harrington
2 SURN Mann
2 _MARNM Mann
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 7 OCT 1864
2 ADDR
3 CITY Blythswood
3 STAE Lanark
3 POST G2 5RL
3 CTRY Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 28 FEB 1937
2 ADDR
3 STAE New York
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Painter
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Harrington Mann 1864-1937
2 CONT
2 CONT Born	7 October 1864 Glasgow Died	28 February 1937 (aged 72) New York Nationality	Scottish Known for	Portraiture Notable work	A Fairy-Tale (1902) Movement	Glasgow Boys Harrington Mann (7 October 1864, Glasgow–28 February 1937, New 
2 CONC York City) was a Scottish portrait artist and decorative painter. He was a member of the Glasgow Boys movement in the 1880s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Art career Mann began his studies at the Glasgow School of Art. He then studied at the Slade School of Fine Art in London under professor Alphonse Legros. He then studied in Paris under the guidance of the figure painters Gustave 
2 CONC Boulanger and Jules-Joseph Lefebvre at the Academie Julian for a short time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mann's early paintings from the 1880s are mainly of fishing communities in Yorkshire. He began to develop a name for himself in portrait painting in the 1890s.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a strong sense of colour and design for decorating interior walls and for stained glass. In the 1890s he designed for the Scottish firm of J. and W. Guthrie (which became Guthrie and Wells). In 1893 he designed the stained 
2 CONC glass for the west window of St Bartholomew's Church, Barbon, in what is now Cumbria.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1900, he moved south to London, also opening a studio in New York, where his paintings became popular. In London, he found success in society portraits, especially of children and including members of the British royal family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mann's use of colour was influenced by James McNeill Whistler. His bold brushwork shows the influence of John Singer Sargent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mann was one of the founder members of the National Portrait Society in 1911.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family He was the second son of John Mann (1827–1910), a chartered accountant, and Mary Newton Harrington (1834–1917), a novelist. John's father was also a painter, John Mann (1797-1827).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mann married the interior decorator Florence Sabine-Pasley (known as Dolly Mann).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mann had three daughters, who appeared in several of his paintings, including Cathleen Sabine, an artist, who married Francis Douglas, 11th Marquess of Queensberry and then J.R. Follett.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Wikipedia Added by Janet Milburn 1/14/18
2 CONT --------------------
