1 NAME William Pickens /Cullum/
2 GIVN William Pickens
2 SURN Cullum
2 _MARNM Cullum
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 11 OCT 1839
2 ADDR
3 CITY Aiken
3 STAE South Carolina
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Son of William Eldridge Cullum (1818-1865) & Frances Elizabeth Gould Merritt (1819-1870).
2 CONT
2 CONT Born near Sandy Springs, Aiken County, married Nacissa virginia Holstein Nov. 18, 1866.
2 CONT
2 CONT "At age 24, he was in the confederate Army and there is a historic record that he re-staffed a flag under heavy gunfire in a battle for which he received a citation. Soon after, he and Narcissa married and moved to the Holston Cro
2 CONC ss Roads section of Saluda County near Batesburg where he was a farmer and did some lumbering on the side. Soon after the birth of their seventh child, about 1885, they moved to Cabot, AR, and tried raising cotton. After five or s
2 CONC ix years, they moved back to South Carolina and settled in Batesburg on a small farm known as the Youngblood place. His chief occupation was as a clerk in his brothers' store (L.D. & J.C. Cullum). In his later years, William devot
2 CONC ed his sales effort to wagons, buggies, and harnesses. He and his family were members of the Baptist Church in Batesburg. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "After the death of his wife, he raised his large family alone and moved closer into town in a home located across from the old Berry Duncan home, which was at one time a school building, and later he moved across town into a prop
2 CONC erty owned by 'Grandpa Gunter' on Mount Willing Road, known as the Baptist Parsonage. He lived there until his death, at which time there still resided with him his two unmarried sons, Merritt and James. His faithful old cook, Bis
2 CONC h, died on the the same day as he (William)."
2 CONT
2 CONT (From The Twin-City News, Batesburg-Leesville, SC, Thursday, May 19, 2011, Remembrances, p. 16).
