1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/5546840/thomas-luther-haman
2 CONT
2 CONT Times Post, Houston, Mississippi, September 3 1942
2 CONT
2 CONT T L Haman Buried Friday At Vaiden
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral rites were held from the Presbyterian church Friday at 1 o'clock, for T L Haman, prominent lawyer, who died early Thursday morning in the Veterans hospital in Memphis, where he was carried ten days before. The service was 
2 CONC conducted by the Rev. J W McNutt, pastor of the Shongalo Presbyterian church of Vaiden, with the Rev. W F Patch, pastor of the Houston Presbyterian church and the Rev. Geo. Williams, pastor of the Methodist church taking part. Bur
2 CONC ial followed in the cemetery at Vaiden with the Rev. E W Ford, of Goodman, lifelong friend of the family assisting Rev. McNutt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Haman died a few days following his 68th birthday. A native of Hinds county, he was the son of Mrs. Adelaide Blanding Haman and the late Rev. Thomas Luther Haman, D D., who was for forty years pastor of the Shongalo church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Haman was graduated from French Camp Academy of which his father was one of the founders and later, at the age of 19, from Center College in Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the Spanish-American war in which he served as corporal with Co. F, Second Mississippi Regiment, he entered the University of Mississippi. He obtained his L L B degree in 1901 and after practicing law for several years i
2 CONC n Pittsboro, he moved to Houston, where he continued his profession until he was carried to Memphis, even though for the last few months he was confined to his home.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1908 Mr. Haman was married to Miss Willie May Clements, of Calhoun county. He was an elder in the Presbyterian church and for years, until a few months ago, taught the men's Sunday School Class. He was a Mason, Knight of Pythia
2 CONC s and a member of the Delta Kappa Epsilon fraternity and a member of the board of trustees of the French Camp Academy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Besides his wife he leaves a daughter, Mrs. John Stephens of Sunflower; his mother, Mrs. T L Haman, of Vaiden; four sisters, Mrs. O M Anderson, of Rolling Fork; Mrs. Chas. Gee, of Carrollton; Misses Mary Elizabeth and Adelaide Ham
2 CONC an, of Vaiden; a brother, Stratford Haman, of Pittsboro and a little granddaughter, Ann Stephens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Active pall bearers were: Cecil Linn, J H Tabb, Fred Dulaney, Wm. Scott, T D Rowlett and Ben Woods.
