1 NAME "Philippe" André Georges Andre /Thurneyssen/
2 GIVN "Philippe" André Georges Andre
2 SURN Thurneyssen
2 _MARNM Thurneyssen
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Saint Michael's Church
2 ADDR
3 CITY Greensboro
3 STAE VT
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1919
2 ADDR
3 CITY Paris
3 STAE Île-de-France
3 CTRY France
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 JUN 2014
2 ADDR
3 CITY Greensboro
3 STAE VT
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Vice-président honoraire
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Philippe Andre' Georges Thurneyssen, of Greensboro, Vt., died peacefully at his home on June 6, 2014. He was 95. He is survived by his younger brother, Claude Thurneyssen (Marie-Louise De Liniers) of Montmorillon, 
2 CONC France; his children: Oliver Thurneyssen (Rebecca Pierce) of Lexington, Ohio, and Joan (Michael Gambro) of Summit, N.J.; grandchildren: Ashton, Andre', Rachel and Caleb Thurneyssen, and Dana, Merrill, and Christopher Gambro; siste
2 CONC r-in-law, Joan Purves Bertaccini of Morris, Conn.; and numerous nieces and nephews in France, the U.S., and Canada.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe was born in Paris, France, on Feb. 19, 1919, the son of Violette Boussod (1889-1977) and Guy Thurneyssen (1888-1970) and younger brother of Lucienne Thurneyssen (1916-1994), who married Pierre de la Grandière. In 1929 his
2 CONC  family moved from Paris to Versailles and summers were spent in nearby Jouy en Josas or on the island of Noirmoutier. Philippe, his siblings and cousins got into all sorts of mischief during those childhood vacations, but cleaned
2 CONC  up nicely for dinner at their grandfather's house each evening, with their best table manners on display. Starting at age 15, Philippe spent several summers in Scotland to perfect his English, an education which certainly helped 
2 CONC him when he arrived in the United States in 1949. Philippe attended business college in Neuchatel, Switzerland for two years until the outbreak of World War II in 1939 when he was drafted into the French Army of the Armistice. Aft
2 CONC er fulfilling his military service, Philippe managed to obtain an identification card signifying him as a farmer. This allowed Philippe to go underground for the French Resistance, and for three years he spied on German airstrips 
2 CONC and worked to spirit Allied pilots to safety through Spain. In August 1944, after the liberation of Paris, Philippe joined the Free French Army as a tank commander under General Leclerc; he and his crew participated in the liberat
2 CONC ion of Strasbourg, followed by the capture of Berchtesgaden in Germany. After the war Philippe worked for the American Express office in Paris and in 1949 came to the United States to work for American Express in New York City. Th
2 CONC ere he met Ellen Purves, of Litchfield, Conn., his wife for 48 years until her death in 1998.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT During the 1950s Philippe did many things to support his young family, including managing a sheep farm in Willsboro, N.Y., and selling foreign sports cars in Torrington, Conn. In 1960 he discovered his true vocation as a French in
2 CONC structor when he started teaching at Indian Mountain School in Lakeville, Conn. Philippe then taught at Mount Hermon School in Gill, Mass. from 1965-1970, and completed his career at Phillips Exeter Academy, in Exeter, N.H., where
2 CONC  he taught for 14 years until his retirement in 1984. From 1961-1972, Philippe spent every summer at Middlebury College in Vermont where he earned his Master's Degree in French Literature, thus completing his college education whi
2 CONC ch had been cut short in 1939.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT — Caledonian Record, 10 June 2014
