1 NOTE {geni:about_me} RICHARD L. DE SAUSSURE, JR. was born December 29, 1917 into a medical family in Macon, Georgia. The family moved to Washington, D.C. where he went to grade school and greatly enjoyed the life in Washington, includi
2 CONC ng trips with his family on the family boat on the Potomac. After graduation from High School, he entered the University of Virginia where he graduated with an A.B. and an M.D. degree in 1942. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His internship was at the University of Virginia Hospital after which he entered the U.S. Army and shortly thereafter joined a Station Hospital north east of London. It was next to the B-17 base and the primary mission was to care
2 CONC  for the airmen. The hospital was on what was called the Buzz Bomb Alley, the direct path from the launch site of the bombs to London. He became a battalion surgeon with the Third Armored Division and traveled with it to Berlin. H
2 CONC e was discharged after three and a half years overseas and returned to the University of Virginia where he was offered a job with Dr. Gayle Crutchfield in Neurosurgery. On completion of the residency in Neurosurgery, he went to th
2 CONC e Kennedy Veteran’s Hospital in Memphis where he consulted frequently with Dr. Eustace Semmes and Dr. Francis Murphey. At their invitation he joined the Semmes-Murphey Clinic, and remained with the Clinic the rest of his professio
2 CONC nal career. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1973 he formed the Mid-South Foundation for Medical Care which grew as a local professional review organization to become responsible for the entire state of Tennessee. He was Chairman of that group and also worked as Medical D
2 CONC irector of the Physician’s Health Care Corporation, working with the physicians associated with the Baptist Memorial Hospital in Memphis. He was Director of the Intern-Resident training program of the Baptist Hospital and coordina
2 CONC ted the training program with the University of Tennessee where he became Professor of Neurosurgery and Assistant Dean. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. DeSaussure was a founding member of the CNS, became President of that organization and represented it to the ABNS. He became Secretary of the ABNS and served on the Board of Directors of the AANS. He was elected President of t
2 CONC he AANS, the first to be President of the CNS and the AANS. He was designated Distinguished Southern Neurosurgeon by the Southern Neurosurgical Society in 1990. He received the Harvey Cushing Medal in 1995. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Since retirement Dr. DeSaussure has served on the vestry of his church, on the Board of Home Healthcare Foundation and on the Board of Directors of the Trezevant Manor Retirement Home. He presently serves on the Board of the Bapti
2 CONC st Memorial College of Health Sciences. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Phyllis Falk during his senior year as resident at the University of Virginia and they have three children: Alexis (Mrs. William David Porter II), Richard Laurens and Denise Anita (Mrs. William Mims Graeber. Dr. and Mrs
2 CONC . DeSaussure also have six grandchildren.
