1 NOTE {geni:about_me} (From the Charleston Post and Courier)
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Charlton deSaussure, who practiced medicine for four decades in Charleston and remained active in the community after his retirement, died Thursday at Roper Hospital. He was 87.
2 CONT
2 CONT DeSaussure was born Dec. 9, 1920, a son of Edward Harleston deSaussure and Eleanor Charlton deSaussure of Charleston. He practiced medicine here from 1950 to 1990.
2 CONT
2 CONT During those years, he combined a private practice in internal medicine with a teaching career at the Medical University of South Carolina as a lecturer in hematology, from 1950 to 1965. After his retirement, the Medical Universit
2 CONC y named deSaussure Professor Emeritus of Medicine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Friends remembered him as dedicated, and as a tough but fair educator. "He was a picture of Charleston," said longtime friend Conyers O'Bryan, who said deSaussure's manner as a doctor was a model for others.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a past president of the Medical Society of South Carolina, a 1942 graduate of Prince-ton University and a 1946 graduate of the Johns Hopkins School of Medicine. Afterward, he trained at Washington University (Barnes Hospita
2 CONC l) in St. Louis.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 2008, MUSC established a medal in his honor, the Omnes Recte Iuvare Society Award, to be given to the graduate who best exemplifies the "compassion, caring, humanity and excellence which for centuries has defined the art of med
2 CONC icine."
2 CONT
2 CONT He belonged to the honorary medical societies of Alpha Omega and Sigma XI.
2 CONT
2 CONT DeSaussure was a member of the Society of the Cincinnati, the St. Cecilia Society and the Carolina Yacht Club.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is survived by his wife of 56 years, Mary Randolph Huger deSaussure; a son, Charlton deSaussure, Jr.; daughters, Mary deSaussure Cutler and Catherine deSaussure Mark; and nine grandchildren. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (From the obituary section of same newspaper)
2 CONT
2 CONT deSAUSSURE, C. Death came, as it must to all men, to Charlton deSaussure, M.D. on November 6, 2008. He will be remembered as a loving husband, father and grandfather, as well as physician, teacher, mentor, genealogist, poker playe
2 CONC r and friend. He was born December 9, 1920 to Edward Harleston and Eleanor Charlton deSaussure. He graduated from Princeton University in 1942 and The Johns Hopkins Medical School in 1945. Afterwards, he trained at Washington Univ
2 CONC ersity School of Medicine (Barnes Hospital) in St. Louis. He served in the United States Army at Walter Reed Hospital and returned to Charleston to begin private practice in 1950. He was a Professor of Medicine at the Medical Univ
2 CONC ersity of South Carolina and past President of the Medical Society of South Carolina. Dr. deSaussure belonged to the Honorary Societies of Alpha Omega & Sigma XI. He was a member of the Society of the Cincinnati, the Saint Cecilia
2 CONC  Society and the Carolina Yacht Club. He is survived by his wife of 56 years, Mary Randolph Huger deSaussure; a son, Charlton deSaussure, Jr. and his wife Ann; a daughter, Mary deSaussure Cutler and her husband Richard, all of Cha
2 CONC rleston; and a daughter, Catherine deSaussure Mark and her husband Chip of Charlotte, North Carolina. He is also survived by nine grandchildren, Mary Cutler Baarcke and her husband Charles, Richard M. Cutler, III, Joseph John Cayl
2 CONC or Mark, III, Charlton deSaussure, III, Caroline Bayard Cutler, Frances Huger deSaussure, Catherine Peronneau Mark, Ann Ehrhardt deSaussure and Eleanor Fleming Mark. He was predeceased by a brother, Major General Edward Harleston 
2 CONC deSaussure, Jr. Services will be held at 2 o'clock Sunday, November 9, 2008 at St. Philip's Episcopal Church, followed by burial in the Churchyard. Friends may call at 34 Meeting Street.
