1 CONT Henry William DeSaussure (1763-1839), born in Pocotaligo, was a lawyer, director of the U.S. Mint, and chancellor of South Carolina. After serving in the Revolutionary War, he was admitted to the South Carolina bar in 1785. For 18
1 CONC  years he was a member of the lower house of the General Assembly and in that capacity took an active part in the establishment of South Carolina College.
1 CONT
1 CONT DeSaussure was appointed director of the U.S. Mint in 1795, a role in which his competent leadership brought about the first coinage of gold. In 1808 he was elected to the chancery bench, a position he held for 29 years and which 
1 CONC was his principal public service. He was acclaimed for his decrees and the compiling and publishing of four volumes of Chancery Reports covering the years from the Revolution to 1817.
0 @N6000000001226963174@ NOTE
1 CONT Henri Louis Frédéric de Saussure (November 27, 1829 Geneva - February 20, 1905 Geneva), was a Swiss mineralogist and entomologist specialising in studies of Hymenoptera and Orthoptera. The mineral saussurite is named after him. He
1 CONC  also was a prolific taxonomist.
1 CONT
1 CONT His elementary education was at Briquet then, at an advanced level at the Institute of Fellenberg. At Fellenberg he was taught by François Jules Pictet de la Rive who introduced him to entomology. After several years study in Pari
1 CONC s where he received the degree of licenciate of the Faculty of Paris and obtaining the degree Doctor from the University of Gießen.
1 CONT
1 CONT He worked mainly on Hymenoptera and Orthoptera. His first paper, in 1852, was on solitary wasps.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1854 he travelled to the West Indies, then to Mexico and the United States of America. There he met Louis Agassiz.
1 CONT
1 CONT He returned to Switzerland in 1856 with collections of American insects, myriapods, crustaceans, birds and mammals.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also interested in geography, geology and ethnology he founded the Geographical Society of Geneva in 1858.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was also a member of the managing committee of the Natural History Museum of Geneva ensuring that it’s collections of Hymenoptera and Orthoptera became one of the best in the world.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1872 he was made an Honorary Fellow of the Entomological Society of London .
0 @N6000000001226963149@ NOTE
1 CONT William Ford De Saussure (February 22, 1792 - March 13, 1870) was a United States Senator from South Carolina. Born in Charleston, he graduated from Harvard University in 1810, studied law, was admitted to the bar and practiced in
1 CONC  Charleston and Columbia. He was a member of the South Carolina House of Representatives in 1846 and a judge of the chancery court in 1847.
1 CONT
1 CONT De Saussure was appointed, and subsequently elected, as a Democrat to the U.S. Senate to fill the vacancy caused by the resignation of R. Barnwell Rhett and served from May 10, 1852, to March 3, 1853; he resumed the practice of la
1 CONC w in Columbia, and was a trustee of South Carolina College (now the University of South Carolina) at Columbia for many years. He died in Columbia in 1870; interment was in Presbyterian Churchyard.
0 @N6000000001226963161@ NOTE
1 CONT Born in Geneva in 1857, Saussure showed early signs of considerable talent and intellectual ability. After a year of studying Latin, Greek, Sanskrit, and a variety of courses at the University of Geneva, he commenced graduate work
1 CONC  at the University of Leipzig in 1876. Two years later at 21 years Saussure studied for a year at Berlin where he wrote his only full-length work, Mémoire sur le système primitif des voyelles dans les langues indo-européenes (Thes
1 CONC is on the Primitive Vowel System in Indo-European Languages). He returned to Leipzig and was awarded his doctorate in 1880. Soon afterwards he relocated to Paris, where he would lecture on ancient and modern languages, and lived f
1 CONC or 11 years before returning to Geneva in 1891. Saussure lectured on Sanskrit and Indo-European at the University of Geneva for the remainder of his life. It was not until 1906 that Saussure began teaching the Course of General Li
1 CONC nguistics that would consume the greater part of his attention until his death in 1913.
1 CONT
1 CONT Saussure's most influential work, Course in General Linguistics (Cours de linguistique générale), was published posthumously in 1916 by former students Charles Bally and Albert Sechehaye on the basis of notes taken from Saussure's
1 CONC  lectures at the University of Geneva. The Course became one of the seminal linguistics works of the 20th century, not primarily for the content (many of the ideas had been anticipated in the works of other 19th century linguists)
1 CONC , but rather for the innovative approach that Saussure applied in discussing linguistic phenomena.
1 CONT
1 CONT Its central notion is that language may be analyzed as a formal system of differential elements, apart from the messy dialectics of realtime production and comprehension. Examples of these elements includes the notion of the lingu
1 CONC istic sign, the signifier, the signified, and the referent.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1996, a manuscript of Saussure's was discovered in his house in Geneva. This text was published as Writings in General Linguistics, and offers significant clarifications of the Course.
